"Internal Error ()\nciteturn29view0 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://crbug.com/1162190\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn29view1 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://git.tizen.org/cgit/platform/upstream/lightweight-web-engine/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n⚙ D106703 [libunwind] adds a way to synthesise libgcc (https://reviews.llvm.org/D106703)\nciteturn29view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 weeks ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://reviews.llvm.org/D106703\",\"lineno\":45}); Total lines: 1144\nL0: cite0†Page Menu cite1†HomePhabricator L1: \nL2: # This is an archive of the discontinued LLVM Phabricator instance.\nL3: \nL4: Paths\nL5: \nL6:   * cite0†Table of Contentst L7: \nL8:   * -libunwind/\nL9:   * 1 CMakeLists.txt\nL10:   * -cmake/\nL11:   * -config-ix.cmake\nL12:   * -docs/\nL13:   * -BuildingLibunwind.rst\nL14:   * 1 gcc_s-aarch64.ver\nL15:   * 1 gcc_s-armv7a.ver\nL16:   * -gcc_s-i386.ver\nL17:   * -gcc_s-x86_64.ver\nL18:   * -src/\nL19:   * -CMakeLists.txt\nL20: \nL21:   * cite0†Hide Panelf L22:   * cite0†Keyboard Reference? L23:  |  | cite2†Differential D106703\nL24: # [libunwind] adds a way to synthesise libgcc Abandonedcite3†Public L25: \nL26: Authored by cite4†cjdb on Jul 23 2021, 1:03 PM.\nL27: \nL28:   * cite5†Download Raw Diff L29: # Details\nL30: Reviewers\nL31: \nL32: cite6†manojgupta L33: cite7†kristof.beyls L34: cite8†phosek L35: cite9†MaskRay L36: cite10†echristo L37: cite11†compnerd L38: Group Reviewers\nL39: \nL40: Restricted Project\nL41: Summary Note: the term \"libgcc\" refers to the all of `libgcc.a`, `libgcc_eh.a`,\nL42: and `libgcc_s.so`. Enabling libunwind as a replacement for libgcc on Linux has proven to be challenging since libgcc_s.so is a required dependency in the cite12†Linux standard base†refspecs.linuxbase.org . Some software is transitively dependent on libgcc because glibc makes hardcoded calls to functions in libgcc_s. For example, the function `__GI___backtrace` eventually makes its way to a cite13†hardcoded dlopen to libgcc_s' _Unwind_Backtrace†github.com .\nL43: Since libgcc and libunwind have the same ABI, but different implementations, the two libraries end up cite14†cross-talking, which ultimately results in a segfault†bugs.chromium.org . To solve this problem, libunwind needs to build a “libgcc” that is, link the necessary functions from compiler-rt and libunwind into an archive and shared object that advertise themselves as `libgcc.a`, `libgcc_eh.a`, and `libgcc_s.so`, so that glibc’s baked calls are diverted to the correct objects in memory.\nL44: Fortunately for us, compiler-rt and libunwind use the same ABI as the libgcc family, so the problem is solvable at the llvm-project configuration level: no program source needs to be edited. Thus, the end result is for a user to configure their LLVM build with a flag that indicates they want to archive compiler-rt/unwind as libgcc.\nL45: We achieve this by compiling libunwind with all the symbols necessary for compiler-rt to emulate the libgcc family, and then generate symlinks named for our \"libgcc\" that point to their corresponding libunwind counterparts. We alternatively considered patching glibc so that the source doesn't directly refer to libgcc, but rather _defaults_ to libgcc, so that a system preferring compiler-rt/libunwind can point to these libraries at the config stage instead.\nL46: Even if we modified the Linux standard base, this alternative won't work because binaries that are built using libgcc will still end up having crosstalk between the differing implementations. This problem has been solved in this manner for cite15†FreeBSD†github.com , and this CL has been tested against cite16†Chrome OS†chromium-review.googlesource.com . This has a dependency on a compiler-rt-aware compiler.\nL47: # Diff Detail\nL48: \nL49: Repository\nL50:     cite17†rG LLVM Github Monorepo L51: ### Event Timeline\nL52: cite4†cjdb created this revision.cite18†Jul 23 2021, 1:03 PM cite19†Herald added a project: Restricted Project. · cite20†View Herald Transcript cite21†Jul 23 2021, 1:03 PM cite19†Herald added a reviewer: Restricted Project. · cite20†View Herald Transcript cite19†Herald added subscribers: cite22†libcxx-commits , cite23†krytarowski , cite24†arichardson and cite25†2 others . · cite20†View Herald Transcript cite4†cjdb requested review of this revision.cite26†Jul 23 2021, 1:03 PM cite19†Herald added a project: Restricted Project.\nL53: · cite27†View Herald Transcript cite28†Jul 23 2021, 1:03 PM cite19†Herald added a subscriber: cite29†llvm-commits . · cite27†View Herald Transcript cite30†Harbormaster completed remote builds in cite31†B115930: Diff 361310 .cite32†Jul 23 2021, 1:06 PM cite4†cjdb edited the summary of this revision.\nL54: cite33†(Show Details) cite34†Jul 23 2021, 1:07 PM cite4†cjdb updated this revision to cite35†Diff 361316 .cite36†Jul 23 2021, 1:10 PM cite0†Comment Actions rebases to reactivate CIcite30†Harbormaster completed remote builds in cite37†B115933: Diff 361316 .cite38†Jul 23 2021, 1:15 PM cite39†efriedma added a subscriber: cite39†efriedma .cite40†Jul 23 2021, 1:44 PM cite0†Comment Actions If I'm understanding correctly, there are two components to this: shared libraries, and static libraries.\nL55: If you have both libgcc_s.so and libunwind.so in the same process, exposing APIs with the same names, the result is confusing at best, sure. Either we need APIs with different names, or libunwind.so needs to replace libgcc_s.so. Ideally, we'd do both, to give users flexibility. But if we're not going to rename the libunwind.so APIs, we shouldn't install libunwind.so at all, since it isn't usable. For the static libraries, I'm not sure what problem you're trying to solve.\nL56: If someone tries to link against both sets of static libraries, that's pretty clearly user error. And the symbols have hidden visibility, so they shouldn't interfere with other shared libraries.cite4†cjdb added a comment.cite41†Jul 23 2021, 1:56 PM cite0†Comment Actions L57: > If I'm understanding correctly, there are two components to this: shared libraries, and static libraries. If you have both libgcc_s.so and libunwind.so in the same process, exposing APIs with the same names, the result is confusing at best, sure. Either we need APIs with different names, or libunwind.so needs to replace libgcc_s.so. Ideally, we'd do both, to give users flexibility. But if we're not going to rename the libunwind.so APIs, we shouldn't install libunwind.so at all, since it isn't usable.\nL58: The problem here isn't that the process wants to use both libgcc_s.so and libunwind.so, but rather that it sometimes \"needs\" to. glibc has a hardcoded dlopens to libgcc_s.so, so anyone using glibc will find libgcc_s.so is inescapable even when using libunwind.so, which rules out both an API rename and replacing libgcc_s.so.\nL59: > For the static libraries, I'm not sure what problem you're trying to solve. If someone tries to link against both sets of static libraries, that's pretty clearly user error. And the symbols have hidden visibility, so they shouldn't interfere with other shared libraries.\nL60: This solves a different part of the crosstalk problem. While trying to migrate Chrome OS from libgcc to compiler-rt and libunwind, I observed that there was crosstalk when statically linking objects too. In this case, I believe the crosstalk is between true libgcc.a and libunwind.so.cite42†dxf added a subscriber: cite42†dxf .cite43†Jul 23 2021, 2:14 PM cite39†efriedma added a comment.cite44†Jul 23 2021, 2:17 PM cite0†Comment Actions L61: > > If I'm understanding correctly, there are two components to this: shared libraries, and static libraries. If you have both libgcc_s.so and libunwind.so in the same process, exposing APIs with the same names, the result is confusing at best, sure. Either we need APIs with different names, or libunwind.so needs to replace libgcc_s.so. Ideally, we'd do both, to give users flexibility. But if we're not going to rename the libunwind.so APIs, we shouldn't install libunwind.so at all, since it isn't usable.\nL62: >\nL63: > The problem here isn't that the process wants to use both libgcc_s.so and libunwind.so, but rather that it sometimes \"needs\" to. glibc has a hardcoded dlopens to libgcc_s.so, so anyone using glibc will find libgcc_s.so is inescapable even when using libunwind.so, which rules out both an API rename and replacing libgcc_s.so.\nL64: I meant that there can only be one \"true\" unwinder, that exposes the libgcc_s.so symbols, and it has to be named libgcc_s.so. Having a libunwind.so installed on the same system exposing the same symbols is just confusing at best, even if it sort of works if they're the same version. So one of the following should be true on a system with a library named libgcc_s.so:\nL65:   1. libunwind.so isn't be installed on the system.\nL66:   2. libunwind.so entry points forward to libgcc_s.so\nL67:   3. libunwind.so exposes APIs with different names, if someone wanted to explicitly use it for some reason. (Not sure if this is a likely use-case.)\nL68: > > For the static libraries, I'm not sure what problem you're trying to solve. If someone tries to link against both sets of static libraries, that's pretty clearly user error. And the symbols have hidden visibility, so they shouldn't interfere with other shared libraries.\nL69: >\nL70: > This solves a different part of the crosstalk problem. While trying to migrate Chrome OS from libgcc to compiler-rt and libunwind, I observed that there was crosstalk when statically linking objects too. In this case, I believe the crosstalk is between true libgcc.a and libunwind.so.\nL71: If there's crosstalk between libgcc.a and libunwind.so, binaries built on systems with true libgcc.a will break on systems with libunwind.so installed as libgcc_s.so. Messing with the names is just covering up the issue.cite4†cjdb added a comment.cite45†Jul 23 2021, 3:07 PM cite0†Comment Actions L72: > > > If I'm understanding correctly, there are two components to this: shared libraries, and static libraries. If you have both libgcc_s.so and libunwind.so in the same process, exposing APIs with the same names, the result is confusing at best, sure. Either we need APIs with different names, or libunwind.so needs to replace libgcc_s.so. Ideally, we'd do both, to give users flexibility. But if we're not going to rename the libunwind.so APIs, we shouldn't install libunwind.so at all, since it isn't usable.\nL73: >>\nL74: >> The problem here isn't that the process wants to use both libgcc_s.so and libunwind.so, but rather that it sometimes \"needs\" to. glibc has a hardcoded dlopens to libgcc_s.so, so anyone using glibc will find libgcc_s.so is inescapable even when using libunwind.so, which rules out both an API rename and replacing libgcc_s.so.\nL75: >\nL76: > I meant that there can only be one \"true\" unwinder, that exposes the libgcc_s.so symbols, and it has to be named libgcc_s.so. Having a libunwind.so installed on the same system exposing the same symbols is just confusing at best, even if it sort of works if they're the same version. So one of the following should be true on a system with a library named libgcc_s.so:\nL77: >\nL78: >   1. libunwind.so isn't be installed on the system.\nL79: >   2. libunwind.so entry points forward to libgcc_s.so\nL80: >   3. libunwind.so exposes APIs with different names, if someone wanted to explicitly use it for some reason. (Not sure if this is a likely use-case.)\nL81: (1) isn't interesting, and I think (3) is a non-starter in this case. (2) sounds to me like you're suggesting libunwind.so be a shell that points to true libgcc_s.so, which is the opposite of this patch. Could you please clarify what you mean here?\nL82: If a vendor builds libunwind using `-DLIBUNWIND_SYNTH_LIBGCC=On`, then that means they want libunwind to be the unwinder and there is some unfortunate circumstance that is requiring libunwind.so to masquerade as libgcc_s.so.\nL83: > > > For the static libraries, I'm not sure what problem you're trying to solve. If someone tries to link against both sets of static libraries, that's pretty clearly user error. And the symbols have hidden visibility, so they shouldn't interfere with other shared libraries.\nL84: >>\nL85: >> This solves a different part of the crosstalk problem. While trying to migrate Chrome OS from libgcc to compiler-rt and libunwind, I observed that there was crosstalk when statically linking objects too. In this case, I believe the crosstalk is between true libgcc.a and libunwind.so.\nL86: >\nL87: > If there's crosstalk between libgcc.a and libunwind.so, binaries built on systems with true libgcc.a will break on systems with libunwind.so installed as libgcc_s.so. Messing with the names is just covering up the issue.\nL88: Some systems (e.g. Chrome OS) don't have this problem because true libgcc.a isn't available.cite6†manojgupta added a comment.cite46†Jul 23 2021, 3:14 PM cite0†Comment Actions L89: \nL90: > If there's crosstalk between libgcc.a and libunwind.so, binaries built on systems with true libgcc.a will break on systems with libunwind.so installed as libgcc_s.so. Messing with the names is just covering up the issue.\nL91: After this patch we'll no longer install true libgcc* libraries on system anymore to avoid any crosstalk issue. llvm's libunwind will be the only one present with anything libgcc* symlinked to libunwind/compiler-rt etc. This way, we will not have any real libgcc dependencies in Chrome OS or crosstalk.cite39†efriedma added a comment.cite47†Jul 23 2021, 4:45 PM cite0†Comment Actions L92: > > So one of the following should be true on a system with a library named libgcc_s.so:\nL93: >>\nL94: >>   1. libunwind.so isn't be installed on the system.\nL95: >>   2. libunwind.so entry points forward to libgcc_s.so\nL96: >>   3. libunwind.so exposes APIs with different names, if someone wanted to explicitly use it for some reason. (Not sure if this is a likely use-case.)\nL97: >\nL98: > (1) isn't interesting, and I think (3) is a non-starter in this case. (2) sounds to me like you're suggesting libunwind.so be a shell that points to true libgcc_s.so, which is the opposite of this patch. Could you please clarify what you mean here?\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nIssue 2963223003: Reland of Switch Chrome on Linux to libc++ - Code Review (https://codereview.chromium.org/2963223003/)\nciteturn29view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://codereview.chromium.org/2963223003/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 689\n"
